  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Placement of a Director General within MHMS in Nov 2022: is already making positive changes within MHMS. This includes improving governance structures to better respond to emerging issues, identifying irrelevant incurred costs, established new partnerships and revitalising the health donor coordination committee (HSCC) meetings which has stopped meeting since the COVID 19 disruption. The HSCC is a forum for discussion on the national health strategic performance, emerging issues and keeping abreast of development partners support to reduce fragmentation, duplication, program management costs and mobilisation. The first health sector coordination committee (HSCC) for 2023 is planned in July 2023.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati Australia Health Sector Program (KAHSP) is an investment delivered in partnership with the Kiribati Ministry of Health and Medical Services. In 2023 KAHSP focused on reinvigorating health sector leadership and coordination following the COVID-19 pandemic. The KAHSP-funded Director General of Health (DG), appointed in 2022, provided strong leadership in 2023 resulting in the finalisation of the National Health Strategic Plan 2024-2027, the first national health plan to be endorsed since 2020, enabling the recommencement of the Health Sector Coordination Meeting and providing strategic direction for health stakeholders to mobilise additional financing for national health priorities. Increased financing covers new hospitals in Betio and Kiritimati Island; a new digital health initiative; and critical workforce planning and development work.  
&#x200d;
The DG&#x2019;s coordination and strategic direction, made possible from Australia&#x2019;s support, provides a strong platform for further health system strengthening work under the new KAHSP II commencing in 2024.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati-Australia Health Support Program incorporates a flexible direct disbursement mechanism to enable the Government of Kiribati to respond to emerging health priorities and communicable disease threats.  In October 2023, this enabled the Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) to mobilise a rapid response to a tuberculosis (TB) outbreak in a boarding school on Abemama island. 
&#x200d;
The MHMS dispatched 11 personnel to Abemama to test and treat TB cases among Chevalier College students.  Further, the MHMS team delivered a comprehensive island-wide TB service including community awareness, refresher TB training for health workers, and TB screening, treatment and prevention. 657 people were reached including 428 students, 37 staff, and 192 community members. 377 were female, 208 male and 76 aged 0-14.  
&#x200d;
MHMS&#x2019;s timely response contained the TB infections, preventing further spread and development of multi-drug resistance cases, and supported Abemama island to better control TB and other communicable disease threats.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Wolbachia Mosquito Program Phase 2 completed January 2023 confirmed the Wolbachia establishment rate of 93.3% in Betio, 90.4% in Bairiki and 28.9% in Nanikaai. This means Betio and Bairiki risks of getting dengue, zika and chikungunya is low unless imported from other villages. The last dengue outbreak (late 2022) confirmed cases were reported from villages that the WMP has not yet reached. The Phase 2 success has made Phase 3 - Translocation of Wolbachia eggs across SouthTarawa from Betio and Bairiki possible. The full establishment of the Wolbachia mosquitoes across S.Tarawa will contribute to the elimination these vector-borne disease.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
